{
 "id": "2015-gjerde-townscape",
 "name": "Townscape Preference Street Elevations",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Study One of Gjerde's (2015) PhD thesis on visual preferences for streetscapes. Participants see six real streets from three British cities and one Norwegian city. Each street is shown as a photographic street elevation, stitched by the author from his own photographs with the buildings labelled A, B, C ..., plus a street-level photograph from each end. For each scene they answer six five-point questions: visual pleasantness; how they feel about the relationship of building heights, architectural styles, materials and finishes, and colours; and their overall preference. They then say whether any building does not fit in the scene and, if so, which one and why. Gender, age band, occupation (architecture/urban design, planning, other ...) and education are asked at the end, as in the thesis form. The thesis analysed per-scene means and the share of positive, neutral and negative answers, correlations and regressions of overall preference on the five characteristics, and comparisons of designers, planners and lay people. Among 191 respondents in the Wellington region, scene 6 (a highly ordered street with a shared arcade) was most preferred (M = 3.44), then scenes 3 (3.23), 1 (3.11) and 4 (3.10), with scenes 2 (2.63) and 5 (2.47) least preferred. Architectural style relationships predicted overall preference best. Source: Morten Gjerde (2015), Street perceptions: A study of visual preferences for New Zealand streetscapes, PhD thesis, Victoria University of Wellington, doi:10.26686/wgtn.17021132, CC BY-SA 4.0. The scene boards are cropped from its Figures 5.3-5.5, and the questions and answer labels are taken from the Appendix A questionnaire. The template will be removed if the author objects. Differences from the original study: (1) the thesis showed the six scenes as A1 boards on a pinboard (in libraries, planning departments and a university atrium) with a paper form; here each scene is shown on screen, one at a time, in the same order; (2) the consent and instruction text is shortened from the form's cover page; (3) the 'which building does not fit' text box is required only when a building is said not to fit; (4) the boards are copies of the thesis figures, so fine detail is lower than on the printed A1 boards; (5) the overall preference label 'Somewhat like' is printed as 'Somewhat like arous-' on the form, a typo corrected here.",
